Java:
Java employs a generational garbage collection algorithm, which divides the memory heap into three generations: young, tenured, and old generation. The young generation holds objects that have recently been allocated, while the tenured generation holds objects that have survived a minor garbage collection cycle. The old generation holds the longest-lived objects.

When an object is created, it is initially allocated in the young generation. If the object survives a minor garbage collection cycle, it is promoted to the tenured generation. If the object survives another garbage collection cycle, it is moved to the old generation.

Garbage collection in Java is performed by the JVM (Java Virtual Machine). The JVM periodically performs garbage collection cycles, which can be triggered by the JVM's internal heuristics or manually by the programmer using the `System.gc()` method. During a garbage collection cycle, the JVM identifies objects that are no longer in use (i.e., no references to them) and reclaims their memory.

